PROCEEDINGS OF THE SECOND DERNDINGLE ENTMOOT
Matter: RFC 0001, rename tree-browser to treebeard
Chair: Fangorn.  Clerk: a birch (the same birch).  Place: Derndingle.

DAY 1 -- F.A. 121-03-01

Dawn.     The Ents come down into the dingle from every side.
          The clerk counts them. It takes until the sun is up.

Morning.  The chair asks that the Entmoot first stand in silence
          for Beechbone, who fell at Isengard on 3019-03-03 and
          who would have had views on a name.

          The Entmoot stands in silence.

Midday.   The Entmoot is still standing in silence.

Evening.  The chair thanks the Entmoot. The silence is entered in
          the record as the whole of the first day's business, and
          the clerk notes that nobody thought it too long.

          Bregalad (Quickbeam) casts his vote. The chair reminds
          him that the question has not yet been put.
          Bregalad says he knows what it will be.

Adjourned at dark.DAY 2 -- F.A. 121-03-02

Morning.  Roll call, with full names.

          The clerk records that the roll call of an Entmoot is not
          a list. Each Ent says his name, and each name is the story
          of that Ent so far, and the stories are long.

Midday.   Roll call continues.

Evening.  Roll call continues. The chair begins his own name.

Night.    The chair's name continues.

Clerk's note: Lindlaure (Summerlong) asks that the word "browser"
be struck from the record as a slur upon beeches. The chair says
it will be struck when it has been debated, and not before.
Lindlaure agrees to wait. He is good at it.DAY 3 -- F.A. 121-03-03

Dawn.     The chair finishes his name.

Morning.  The chair reads the RFC aloud. The motivation is heard:
          a young hobbit, carried through the forest on 3019-03-01,
          called the tool "a tree browser, like?" and the name was
          written down before anyone could say hoom.

          Long murmur. Several hooms. The clerk cannot count them.

Midday.   Carnimir (Rowanred) rises to second Bregalad's vote of
          day 1. It is pointed out that a vote cannot be seconded.
          Carnimir says he will second it anyway, in case.

Evening.  The chair explains why the old name was short.
          (This explanation is itself not short. See day 4.)DAY 4 -- F.A. 121-03-04

All day.  The chair, continuing: what a name is.

          A name is not a label. A label says what a thing is for.
          A name says what a thing is, and how it came to be that,
          and it grows as the thing grows. The Ents' own names are
          very long, and they are not finished.

          The tool, the chair says, is young. It should not have a
          long name yet, for it has not lived long enough to earn
          one. But it should have a name, and not a description.

Galathorn (Greenthorn) asks whether the name has been sung. It has
not. He says he will not vote on a name that has not been sung.
The clerk marks him: abstain (pending song).

The chair says hoom, which the clerk records as sympathy.DAY 5 -- F.A. 121-03-05

Morning.  Alternatives are read (RFC 0001, section 5).

          1. keep "tree-browser"    -- no seconder
          2. the full Entish name   -- the chair begins to say it;
                                       withdrawn at midday by the
                                       chair himself, still speaking
          3. "ent" (Bregalad)       -- too short; the lint says so
          4. "hoom"                 -- a remark from the back
          5. "huorn"                -- nobody saw who proposed it
          6. "treebeard"            -- to be debated

Afternoon. Doronbrun (Brownoak) begins to speak for the oaks.

Evening.  Doronbrun is speaking for the oaks.DAY 6 -- F.A. 121-03-06

Morning.  Doronbrun is speaking for the oaks.

11:00     Fladrif (Skinbark) arrives from the high slopes, where he
          has lived since the orcs came with their axes. He walks
          slowly and with care. The Entmoot makes room.

          It is the first Entmoot he has attended in an Age. The
          clerk records that nobody said anything about that, which
          was the kindest thing to say.

Afternoon. Fladrif asks for the question. It is read to him.

Evening.  Doronbrun is still speaking for the oaks. The chair asks
          whether the oaks have reached a view. Doronbrun says the
          oaks are still considering whether to have one.DAY 7 -- F.A. 121-03-07

Morning.  Mornadu (Darkshade), of the firs, rises against.

          He says: every name in the forest has been changed once
          too often. Isengard became the Treegarth of Orthanc, and
          that was just. But a thing that is renamed every Age is
          a thing nobody can find.

          The chair agrees this is so.

          Mornadu says he will vote for the old name, so that there
          will be at least one vote for it, and so that the record
          will show it was not thrown away without a word.

          The Entmoot says hoom, together. The clerk records this as
          respect, and not as agreement.

16:30     Fladrif votes: treebeard. He returns to the slopes.DAY 8 -- F.A. 121-03-08

Morning.  Tathardol (Willowhead), of the willows, speaks.

          He speaks of the Old Forest across the mountains, and of
          the willow there that is grey and old and not kind; and of
          the one who lives beside it, who is older than any tree
          and has never needed a name longer than his own.

          The chair notes that this is the first remark of the
          Entmoot to argue for a *short* name. It is entered with
          care. (Cross-ref: 03_old-forest.)

Afternoon. Tathardol continues about willows in general.

Night.    The clerk falls asleep. The clerk is a birch; this is
          not a dereliction. The record resumes at dawn.DAY 9 -- F.A. 121-03-09

Morning.  The question of section 7: is "treebeard" a name?

          The chair speaks.

          "Treebeard is what the hobbits call me. It is a short name
          and a quick one, and it does not tell my story. But it was
          given kindly, by people who had little time, and I have
          answered to it gladly. The tool is used by people in a
          hurry. Let them have a name that is also in a hurry, and
          let the forest keep its real one."

          Silence of about an hour. Nobody says hoom.

Dawn/dusk. Finglas (Leaflock) is visited where he stands. He has
          been treeish since the Third Age began and has not come to
          an Entmoot since. The question is put to him. By dusk of
          day 11 he has rustled once. It is entered as a yes.DAY 10 -- F.A. 121-03-10

Morning.  Gwathlaeg (Ashgrey) says he will accept any name longer
          than three letters, and would like to go home.
          The chair says the first part is noted.

Midday.   The command. It is observed that the command is "tb",
          which is two letters, and which src/entish.rs rejects.

          Long debate. The ruling (RFC 0001, section 6):

            "tb" is not a name. It is a call: the noise you make
             across a glade to turn someone round. Calls may be
             short. Names may not.

          An exemption is written into treebeard.toml.

Evening.  Bregalad asks whether he may now vote. He is told that
          he voted on day 1. He says he knows, and would like to
          again, for the pleasure of it.DAY 11 -- F.A. 121-03-11

Dawn.     A Huorn is found standing in the tally-glade. Nobody saw
          it arrive. It has, in some fashion, marked the tally for
          "treebeard". The chair rules that Huorns do not vote, and
          that the mark shall be recorded and not counted.

          The Huorn does not move.

Morning.  The oaks are asked again. Doronbrun is still speaking.

Dusk.     Finglas rustles.

Night.    The tally is read so far (entmoot/name-vote.csv):

             treebeard       8
             tree-browser    1   (Mornadu, for the record)
             abstain         1   (Galathorn, pending song)
             pending         1   (the oaks)
             not counted     1   (a Huorn)
DAY 12 -- F.A. 121-03-12

Dawn.     The chair rises. The whole of the dingle is quiet.

          "The Entmoot has heard every voice that wished to speak,
           and some that are still speaking. The oaks will answer
           in their own time, and their answer will be kept.

           The tool shall be called treebeard. The call shall stay
           tb. The old name shall be kept in the record, so that
           Mornadu's vote is not lost, and so that anyone who looks
           for the old name will find their way here.

           We have decided."

          The Ents go back up out of the dingle, slowly, in every
          direction. The Huorn remains.

Clerk:    Proceedings closed. RFC 0001: accepted.
          Galathorn has begun to compose the song.
          Doronbrun is speaking for the oaks.

                                      -- the clerk (a birch)
